      "displayName": "Jared Whiklo",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=6089a4c12a753d0071341edb",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>So I also did some digging.</p>\n\n<p>According to the XML Schema : Datatypes specification. Section <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#namespaces\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">3.1 Namespace Considerations</a> defines the namespace URI as <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#built-in-datatypes\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\",</a> but in Section <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#built-in-datatypes\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">3 Built-in Datatypes</a> they specify that when using the prefix for (in their example) an \"int\" it would appear as \"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int\"</p>\n\n<p>In Fedora this is coming from Jena. Because we set the created and lastModified datetimes we set them using the Calendar type. This makes Jena see them as <a href=\"https://jena.apache.org/documentation/javadoc/jena/org/apache/jena/datatypes/xsd/XSDDateTime.html\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">XSDDateTime</a> datatypes. So they get the internally defined namespace.</p>\n\n<p>We could go to Jena and ask, but I imagine they would point to the XML Schema documents (linked above). So we could go to the W3C and ask...or just add # to the end.</p>\n\n<p>For reference:</p>\n\n<p>In the <a href=\"https://github.com/fcrepo4/fcrepo4/blob/master/fcrepo-kernel-modeshape/src/main/java/org/fcrepo/kernel/modeshape/rdf/impl/mappings/PropertyToTriple.java#L81\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PropertyToTriple</a> converter we call the <a href=\"https://github.com/fcrepo4/fcrepo4/blob/master/fcrepo-kernel-modeshape/src/main/java/org/fcrepo/kernel/modeshape/rdf/converters/ValueConverter.java#L84-L109\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">ValueConverter</a>. Because we pass in strings they are considered default datatypes and fall through to the <a href=\"https://github.com/fcrepo4/fcrepo4/blob/master/fcrepo-kernel-modeshape/src/main/java/org/fcrepo/kernel/modeshape/rdf/converters/ValueConverter.java#L148-L160\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">stringliteral2node</a> which is able to pull the datatype off but only recognizes \"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#datetime\" as specifying a datetime. So you can't do range comparisons unless you use the #.</p>",
